1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,348
Welcome to Episode Seven, an extended lesson on Daily Bias, focused on the draw on liquidity.

2
00:00:07,768 --> 00:00:15,520
We will map liquidity, decide which objective matters most, and then combine bias with PO3 and Kill Zones.

3
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:23,004
Before you choose an objective, map everything. Start with the previous day high and low.

4
00:00:23,424 --> 00:00:29,208
Add the previous week high and low. Here, last week’s high is also the most recent swing high.

5
00:00:29,628 --> 00:00:33,348
Mark earlier swing highs and lows that have not been taken.

6
00:00:33,768 --> 00:00:41,496
Then separate internal liquidity, such as imbalances inside the range, from external liquidity at the range edges.

7
00:00:43,316 --> 00:00:46,700
Equal highs and equal lows deserve a closer look.

8
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:55,064
Here two four-hour highs print at exactly the same price. That is a genuine equal high, with buy-side liquidity above.

9
00:00:55,484 --> 00:01:02,036
Lower down, two lows match exactly: equal lows, with sell-side liquidity below.

10
00:01:02,456 --> 00:01:06,680
Because they are obvious, many traders place stops just beyond them.

11
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:12,956
But roughly similar highs, far apart in price, are not equal highs. Be strict.

12
00:01:14,776 --> 00:01:22,048
How do we prioritise? First, alignment with the bias. A bullish bias puts buy-side objectives first.

13
00:01:22,468 --> 00:01:30,484
Second, significance. A weekly level usually outranks a daily one, and a daily level outranks an intraday one.

14
00:01:30,904 --> 00:01:35,560
Third, realistic reach. Is the level within a normal day’s range?

15
00:01:35,980 --> 00:01:39,652
Fourth, cleanliness. Has the level already been raided?

16
00:01:40,072 --> 00:01:50,488
For example: with a bullish bias, the untouched previous day high may be today’s realistic objective, while the previous week high is the larger draw for the week.

17
00:01:52,308 --> 00:02:00,972
A useful mental model: price often moves from one external liquidity pool toward the other, passing through internal liquidity on the way.

18
00:02:01,392 --> 00:02:09,000
Here, after sell-side liquidity is taken, displacement leaves internal liquidity behind, a fair value gap.

19
00:02:09,420 --> 00:02:14,724
The objective, defined in advance, is buy-side liquidity above the old high.

20
00:02:15,144 --> 00:02:19,920
Price reaches it, but not in a straight line. There are pauses and pullbacks.

21
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:30,828
Once the objective is reached, behaviour may change: consolidation or reversal. That is the moment to reassess, not to extend the target automatically.

22
00:02:31,248 --> 00:02:36,576
A bearish bias follows the same logic with sell-side liquidity as the objective.

23
00:02:38,396 --> 00:02:48,380
Now combine Daily Bias with PO3. On the left, the daily context: bullish structure, discount, and the previous day high as the objective.

24
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:54,032
On the right, today. Mark the daily open; accumulation forms around it.

25
00:02:54,452 --> 00:02:58,820
Manipulation trades below the open, against the bullish bias.

26
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:02,816
Distribution then expands toward the daily objective.

27
00:03:03,236 --> 00:03:12,620
PO3 is one possible interpretation of how a bullish day is delivered. The bias itself still comes from the higher-timeframe context.

28
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:22,048
Now combine bias with timing. The higher-timeframe bias is bullish, with the previous day high as the objective.

29
00:03:22,468 --> 00:03:29,068
During the London window, price sweeps the Asian low, a liquidity interaction against the bias.

30
00:03:29,488 --> 00:03:34,720
Displacement and a market structure shift follow: lower-timeframe confirmation.

31
00:03:35,140 --> 00:03:38,452
In the New York window, price reaches the objective.

32
00:03:38,872 --> 00:03:51,496
Notice the order of reasoning. The bias came first, from structure and liquidity. The Kill Zone only told us when to watch for evidence. A Kill Zone does not determine the Daily Bias by itself.

33
00:03:53,316 --> 00:03:58,932
To summarise. Map all liquidity first, and separate internal from external.

34
00:03:59,352 --> 00:04:07,800
Prioritise objectives by bias, significance, reach and cleanliness, and expect a stepped path rather than a straight line.

35
00:04:08,220 --> 00:04:16,836
PO3 is a lens on how the day may be delivered within the bias. Kill Zones time the evidence, but they do not set the bias.

36
00:04:18,656 --> 00:04:24,392
Time to check your understanding. Pause the video and answer these five questions in your own words.

37
00:04:26,312 --> 00:04:31,736
Write down your reasoning, not just a one-word answer. The reasoning is what we assess.

38
00:04:35,556 --> 00:04:41,772
